\section{Policy on n_tracts_num and n_tracts_den}{

These two integer columns expose the per-tract contribution counts
separately for each rate's numerator and denominator ACS codes, surfacing
ACS-suppression asymmetry that the single \code{n_tracts} slot cannot represent.

Population rules:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Source-variable rows} (every row passed through from
\code{\link[=cacs_propagate_moe]{cacs_propagate_moe()}}): both \code{n_tracts_num} and \code{n_tracts_den} are
\code{NA_integer_}. The \code{n_tracts} column already carries the per-variable
tract count for these rows; the two new columns are reserved for
derived rates only.
\item \strong{Derived-rate rows} (the appended rate rows for
\code{poverty_rate}, \code{snap_rate}, \code{ssi_rate}, \code{unemp_rate},
\code{labor_force_participation}): both columns are populated
\strong{independently} from the carrier-tibble count of contributing tracts
for the numerator ACS code and the denominator ACS code respectively.
\item \strong{Carrier-missing edge case} on a derived-rate row (the
\code{failure_origin == "carrier"} branch): both columns collapse to
\code{NA_integer_}. The diagnostic surface for that condition is
\code{failure_origin}, not the count columns.
}

The two counts are reported separately rather than collapsed to a single
\code{min(num, den)} because the numerator and denominator of a curated rate
often come from separately suppressed ACS table cells. For example,
\code{ssi_rate} divides \code{B19056_002} (households with Supplemental Security
Income) by \code{B19056_001} (the \code{B19056} total-household universe), and a
catchment can have different valid tract contributions for those two cells.
A bivariate exposure lets the caller spot that asymmetry directly; a
collapsed value would hide it.

The \code{n_tracts} column itself remains \code{NA_integer_} on rate rows; only the
two \verb{_num} / \verb{_den} slots carry rate-row tract counts.
}

\section{Rate definitions and optional bounds audit}{

\code{ssi_rate} follows the canonical ACS \code{B19056} formula
\code{B19056_002 / B19056_001}: households with Supplemental Security Income
divided by the \code{B19056} total-household universe.

Set \code{options(catchmentACS.audit_rates = TRUE)} to enable a warning-only
debug audit against the sanctioned rate bounds
(\code{poverty_rate} \verb{[0, 0.6]}, \code{snap_rate} \verb{[0, 0.5]}, \code{ssi_rate}
\verb{[0, 0.15]}, \code{unemp_rate} \verb{[0, 0.30]}, and \code{labor_force_participation}
\verb{[0.3, 0.85]}). These bounds are diagnostics, not hard validity rules.
}

\section{Carrier-missing warnings}{

A derived rate requires both numerator and denominator carrier rows for
the same (\code{site_id}, \code{drive_time_min}) cell. The default ACS variable
catalogue includes every carrier required by the five sanctioned rates.
Direct pipelines or explicit custom variable sets that omit a carrier
still return the affected rate row with \code{estimate = NA}, \code{moe = NA}, and
\code{failure_origin = "carrier"}, and emit a
\code{catchmentACS_warning_carrier_missing} condition. That warning also
inherits from \code{catchmentACS_warning_runtime} for backward-compatible
warning capture.
}

